1d:T536,{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BlogPosting","@id":"https://www.outbrick.site/blog/monument-valley-less-game-more-experience/#article","mainEntityOfPage":{"@type":"WebPage","@id":"https://www.outbrick.site/blog/monument-valley-less-game-more-experience"},"headline":"Monument Valley and the power of making less feel complete","description":"A closer look at ustwo’s “less game, more experience” idea—and what OutBrick borrows from it.","image":["https://www.outbrick.site/blog/monument-valley-less-game-more-experience.png"],"datePublished":"2026-09-04","dateModified":"2026-09-04","author":{"@type":"Person","name":"OutBrick Editorial Desk","description":"The OutBrick Editorial Desk turns player research, accessibility practice, and game-making lessons into useful, readable notes."},"publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":"OutBrick","url":"https://www.outbrick.site","logo":{"@type":"ImageObject","url":"https://www.outbrick.site/icon.png"}},"articleSection":"Success stories","keywords":"Monument Valley, mobile games, UX, puzzle design","citation":["https://ustwo.com/work/monument-valley/","https://www.gdcvault.com/play/1021380/Designing-Monument-Valley-Less-Game","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6453078/"]}14:["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"$1d"}}]
15:["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"BreadcrumbList\",\"itemListElement\":[{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":1,\"name\":\"OutBrick\",\"item\":\"https://www.outbrick.site\"},{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":2,\"name\":\"Journal\",\"item\":\"https://www.outbrick.site/blog\"},{\"@type\":\"ListItem\",\"position\":3,\"name\":\"Monument Valley and the power of making less feel complete\",\"item\":\"https://www.outbrick.site/blog/monument-valley-less-game-more-experience\"}]}"}}]
16:["$","section","a-screen-can-be-a-place",{"id":"a-screen-can-be-a-place","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"A screen can be a place"}],[["$","p","ustwo’s project page describes Monument Valley as a short, complete experience: novel mechanics without grind, failure pressure, collectible stars, or leaderboards. The studio’s stated ambition was for players to finish the whole game on mobile, from beginning to end.",{"children":"ustwo’s project page describes Monument Valley as a short, complete experience: novel mechanics without grind, failure pressure, collectible stars, or leaderboards. The studio’s stated ambition was for players to finish the whole game on mobile, from beginning to end."}],["$","p","That first-party account explains intention rather than measuring causality. It does not prove that any single design choice caused the game’s success. But it gives us a clear design stance: a mobile game can be designed as a sequence of authored moments instead of an endless demand for more time.",{"children":"That first-party account explains intention rather than measuring causality. It does not prove that any single design choice caused the game’s success. But it gives us a clear design stance: a mobile game can be designed as a sequence of authored moments instead of an endless demand for more time."}]],null,null,["$","p",null,{"className":"article-source-note","children":[["$","span",null,{"children":"Research trail"}],[["$","a","monument-valley-ustwo",{"href":"#reference-monument-valley-ustwo","aria-label":"Jump to source: ustwo (n.d.)","children":["[",1,"]"]}]]]}]]}]
17:["$","section","less-game-more-experience",{"id":"less-game-more-experience","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Less game, more experience"}],[["$","p","The GDC session description for lead designer Ken Wong connects M. C. Escher’s impossible architecture with ustwo’s background as a UX studio. Each screen was treated as a piece of art, and the team rethought the game as a user experience rather than a test of difficulty alone.",{"children":"The GDC session description for lead designer Ken Wong connects M. C. Escher’s impossible architecture with ustwo’s background as a UX studio. Each screen was treated as a piece of art, and the team rethought the game as a user experience rather than a test of difficulty alone."}],["$","p","That shift is useful for puzzle makers. Difficulty is only one dimension of value. A board can also be memorable because it has a strong silhouette, a small surprise, or a moment where the player sees the rule from a new angle. OutBrick’s deep indigo shell and toy-like materials are there to make those moments feel held, not hurried.",{"children":"That shift is useful for puzzle makers. Difficulty is only one dimension of value. A board can also be memorable because it has a strong silhouette, a small surprise, or a moment where the player sees the rule from a new angle. OutBrick’s deep indigo shell and toy-like materials are there to make those moments feel held, not hurried."}]],null,null,["$","p",null,{"className":"article-source-note","children":[["$","span",null,{"children":"Research trail"}],[["$","a","monument-gdc-2014",{"href":"#reference-monument-gdc-2014","aria-label":"Jump to source: Wong (2014)","children":["[",2,"]"]}]]]}]]}]
18:["$","section","complete-does-not-mean-easy",{"id":"complete-does-not-mean-easy","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Complete does not mean easy"}],[["$","p","A complete experience can still ask for concentration. The important difference is whether the player can understand the purpose of the challenge and whether the game respects the player after the challenge is over.",{"children":"A complete experience can still ask for concentration. The important difference is whether the player can understand the purpose of the challenge and whether the game respects the player after the challenge is over."}],["$","p","OutBrick lets a board be knotty without making the whole app feel hostile. Undo and wedge recovery keep the emotional arc attached to solving, not to proving that a mistake deserves a penalty. The board can be difficult. The relationship does not have to be.",{"children":"OutBrick lets a board be knotty without making the whole app feel hostile. Undo and wedge recovery keep the emotional arc attached to solving, not to proving that a mistake deserves a penalty. The board can be difficult. The relationship does not have to be."}]],null,null,["$","p",null,{"className":"article-source-note","children":[["$","span",null,{"children":"Research trail"}],[["$","a","monument-valley-ustwo",{"href":"#reference-monument-valley-ustwo","aria-label":"Jump to source: ustwo (n.d.)","children":["[",1,"]"]}],["$","a","flow-review-2019",{"href":"#reference-flow-review-2019","aria-label":"Jump to source: Ma et al. (2019)","children":["[",3,"]"]}]]]}]]}]
19:["$","section","the-outbrick-translation",{"id":"the-outbrick-translation","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"The OutBrick translation"}],[["$","p","For OutBrick, “less game” means less friction between the player and the idea of the board. One screen can be enough. One move can be enough to teach a rule. One clear can be enough to end a session on a good note.",{"children":"For OutBrick, “less game” means less friction between the player and the idea of the board. One screen can be enough. One move can be enough to teach a rule. One clear can be enough to end a session on a good note."}],["$","p","The aim is not to make a smaller version of someone else’s success. It is to make a world in which a small puzzle feels complete on its own terms.",{"children":"The aim is not to make a smaller version of someone else’s success. It is to make a world in which a small puzzle feels complete on its own terms."}]],null,null,["$","p",null,{"className":"article-source-note","children":[["$","span",null,{"children":"Research trail"}],[["$","a","monument-gdc-2014",{"href":"#reference-monument-gdc-2014","aria-label":"Jump to source: Wong (2014)","children":["[",2,"]"]}],["$","a","monument-valley-ustwo",{"href":"#reference-monument-valley-ustwo","aria-label":"Jump to source: ustwo (n.d.)","children":["[",1,"]"]}]]]}]]}]
